\Section{What the comparison can establish}
The model and each baseline use the same withheld outcome cells. Compare their errors in the stated units and at the same forecast horizon. A better retained-count forecast need not produce the better revenue forecast, and predictive performance does not establish a campaign's causal effect.
\Boundary{The future challenge covers the next 12 calendar months for the already acquired cohorts. This differs from the executive report's valuation horizon of each cohort's first 12 monthly receipts. Hidden future outcomes are reserved for scoring and do not enter these historical checks.}

\NewPage
\PageTitle{A6 / HISTORICAL VALUATION CHECK}{Show exactly how much\newline of each value was forecast}{Technical appendix | Training includes the calendar month beginning \VAR{valuation_backtest_origin}; later calendar months are used only for scoring.}
\VAR{valuation_backtest_sentence}
\TableCaption{valuation-backtest}{Cohort-level historical value and payback forecasts, separating already observed recovery from forecast recovery.}
\VAR{valuation_backtest_table}
\Evidence{Saved model output: \Code{valuation_backtest.csv}. LTV values are discounted contribution before blended CAC, per original acquired customer. ``Observed / forecast'' counts the receipts within the common 12-month horizon; payback month counts receipts from 1.}
\Section{Why total-value and forecast-tail errors are the same}
Observed contribution is copied identically into the actual and predicted totals. The forecast covers only the remaining months. Consequently, total LTV error equals the error in the unobserved tail; the long observed prefix does not create additional predictive accuracy. Rows are ordered by the amount of history available at the origin.
\Section{The baseline and its weakest-information case}
The naive baseline freezes the last three observed renewal rates and revenue per retained customer. A cohort with only its acquisition month has no observed renewal rate; the baseline uses an explicit 0.8 renewal fallback. Its value is an assumption, not an estimated renewal rate. The December row makes this low-information comparison visible.
\Section{What the payback forecast represents}
Forecast payback is the first crossing of acquisition cost by the \textbf{posterior-mean expected contribution path}. It is not the mean or median of a payback-time distribution, and it does not estimate the chance that a realized customer cohort recovers its cost by that month. Posterior parameter bands also exclude future customer-level process noise.
\Boundary{The inference model is an approximation to observed customer behavior, not access to the process that created the source records. The small set of future-payback cases is a narrow diagnostic: interpret zero month error, if observed, alongside full value errors and incomplete histories. The future competition holdout remains private and is not part of these historical validation results.}

